      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "Using the vdW‑DF‑OptB88 functional for all dimensionalities yields accurate lattice parameters and formation energies for both van‑der‑Waals and non‑vdW bonded materials."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00220",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "The first RELAX calculation is performed in collinear spin-polarized fashion with initial magnetic moments set to 1.0 µB per atom, and spin polarization is turned off in subsequent calculations if magnetization is below 0.025 µB/atom."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00520",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "The atomic cluster expansion (ACE) is generalized to model scalar, vectorial and tensorial atomic properties and to include additional atomic degrees of freedom—species, magnetic moments, and charges—on equal footing with atomic positions."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00522",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "At the lowest order, the magnetic ACE reproduces a distance‑dependent Heisenberg interaction."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00523",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "Representing tensors with spherical harmonics yields a dramatically sparser basis, where a rank‑N Cartesian tensor with 3^N components can be represented by (N+1)^2 spherical harmonics (e.g., a 10th‑order tensor requires 121 harmonics instead of 59 049 Cartesian components)."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00524",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "Moments Tensor Potentials (MTP) and the Spectral Neighbor Analysis Potential (SNAP) can be expressed exactly as special cases of the ACE."
    },
    {
      "id": "clai_00529",
      "type": "claim",
      "surface_form": "Because the ACE basis is complete and hierarchical, it can in principle achieve arbitrary accuracy for any scalar, vectorial or tensorial property."
    },
